Advanced Multiple Sclerosis
Palliative & End of Life
Severe permanent spasticity, Irreversible paralysis, Severe bulbar dysfunction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-006
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of painful spasticity and intractable neuropathic pain in a completely immobile patient unable to swallow regular medications.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, formal disability support worker
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the sudden onset of intractable, intensely burning neuropathic pain and a severe spasticity crisis causing irreversible joint contractures to lock in a rigid, agonizing position. The patient was in extreme distress and could not be safely given oral rescue medications due to severe, progressive bulbar dysfunction. Urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally required to administer parenteral muscle relaxants and analgesia to prevent profound psychological trauma, tissue damage, and respiratory compromise prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Patient communicated via spelling board: "Legs locked tight. Face burning. Can't swallow pills without choking."
History of Present Illness: Over the past several years, the patient has experienced profound lower extremity paralysis predictably progressing to complete quadriplegia. Recently, severe bulbar dysfunction characterized strictly by progressive, profound dysphagia and dysarthria has made oral intake highly hazardous. Over the last six hours, the patient developed a severe spasticity crisis. Severe, permanent, and generalized spasticity directly caused painful, completely irreversible joint contractures to tighten aggressively. This is compounded by an acute flare of intractable, intensely burning neuropathic pain affecting the trigeminal distribution. A neurogenic bladder is managed with a suprapubic catheter, which the carer reports is draining normally. The patient is experiencing severe, intractable chronic fatigue and advanced cognitive decline is minimally present but communication is intact via board.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound, complete quadriplegia, entirely dependent on ceiling hoists for transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of aspiration with any oral intake; high risk of pressure injuries due to fixed contractures.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient is in visible, extreme distress, diaphoresic, with rigid lower and upper limbs and profound facial grimacing.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals hyperreflexia, sustained bilateral ankle clonus, and severe permanent spasticity with irreversible paralysis in all four limbs (Modified Ashworth Scale 4). Cranial nerve exam shows hyperesthesia over the V2/V3 distribution of the right trigeminal nerve. Oral inspection reveals pooling of saliva and severe bulbar dysfunction with an absent gag reflex. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the rigid, contracted limbs (spasticity/paralysis) and pooling secretions (bulbar dysfunction).
Environmental Observations: Hospital bed is appropriately configured; ceiling hoist is present. Communication board is positioned correctly.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral baclofen and carbamazepine doses were missed earlier today due to severe coughing and choking during administration.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Multiple Sclerosis - Status: Exacerbated (Spasticity/Neuralgia Crisis)
Differentials:

Blocked urinary catheter (Autonomic Dysreflexia trigger) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the suprapubic catheter actively draining 400ml of clear, dilute urine into the bag without lower abdominal distension.

Acute aspiration pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, clear bilateral lung fields on auscultation, and no purulent sputum production.

Deep vein thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely as the leg rigidity and pain are bilateral, symmetrical, and lacking unilateral acute swelling or distinct erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, agonizing spasticity and neuropathic pain crisis triggered directly by missed oral medications secondary to advancing, severe bulbar dysfunction. Objective clinical signs include severe tachycardia, hypertension (driven by uncontrolled pain), and profound tetanic rigidity of the limbs. Because the patient's severe dysphagia entirely precludes the safe administration of compensatory oral medications, delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would force the patient to endure hours of unrelenting, excruciating pain and risk muscle tearing or severe skin breakdown from sustained contractures. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was essential to bypass the oral route and administer prompt intramuscular and subcutaneous symptom relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intramuscular midazolam for acute severe spasticity and subcutaneous ketamine for refractory neuropathic pain. Placed an urgent referral to the specialist palliative care team to convert oral baclofen and carbamazepine to a continuous subcutaneous infusion via syringe driver.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the facial nerve pain beginning to tingle (trigger), you must indicate this on your communication board to your carer immediately (behavior), so they can administer the fast-acting buccal pain spray before the pain peaks (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent community palliative care nursing review for syringe driver implementation tomorrow morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of specialized soft positioning wedges to protect contracted joints from pressure injuries.
Follow-up: Palliative care nursing review in 12 hours; GP review in 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops respiratory depression (breathing less than 10 breaths per minute), becomes completely unresponsive, or if the severe spasticity and pain remain uncontrolled 60 minutes after the injectable medications, the carer must contact the after-hours medical service or dial 000 for emergency transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Medication adjustments are strictly aimed at acute symptom control; the underlying neurological deficits are permanent and irreversible.
Clinical Course: The ability to swallow will continue to decline permanently, making injectable or patch medications the primary, non-negotiable route moving forward.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to give any further oral medications or fluids. If the patient chokes on their own saliva and turns blue, perform gentle oral suctioning and call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because swallowing has become too dangerous and is causing you to miss critical doses, we are switching to medications that go directly into the muscle or under the skin to rapidly unlock your legs and stop the nerve pain safely.
Actionable Steps: Use the communication board early to report pain. Allow the hoist and wedges to fully support your limbs rather than fighting the stiffness.

